 higher one upping your  accomplishment it lands on a tiny branch  above you reaching the actual tippy top  that you couldn't reach and it's then  you realize that the feeling you're  chasing isn't just so that you can look  down on the earth and scoff but so you  can look up at the world and wonder so  you spend your life with your chin to  the sky feet following your gaze  everything you do every action you take  is in the name of elevation you chose a  career in being a union line man you  work the night shifts climbing up  telephone poles your happiest farthest  from the ground every november you  vacation to new york where you run up  the empire state building just to taste  new heights you summit 86 flights  1576 stairs but no matter what extremes  you go to you feel like there is  something up there still out of reach  waiting for you there is only one thing  that will satisfy you mount everest if  you can just reach the summit you  promise yourself your wife your god and  your family that you'll stop straining  your neck upward that you'll enjoy what  is in plain sight that you'll settle  down and live the life that's right  there in front of you you begin to study  for your two-month climb you learn about  the sub-zero temperatures you learn  about the journey's physical demands on  your body you learn about navigation  communication and preparation but what  you don't learn about or rather can't  learn about is the anomaly that awaits  you unfortunately some lessons are  omitted from books left to be learned  only by experience and by that time i'm  afraid it'll be much too late in the  lecture to get a passing grade the day  comes and you begin your mission to the  mountain's peak there's something  otherworldly about it the way you move  up this mountain it isn't just a matter  of labor there's a spirituality within  the journey you feel as if you're  floating being pushed toward the heavens  by some generous being behind you but  what is it that actually pushes you  upward could it be as simple an answer  as will power determination skill no  you've felt those before and while they  have surely powered many men to do great  things this force is different it is not  so much that it is pushing you forward  it is more like it is pulling you up or  rather luring you in you're amazed at  how effortless the journey is at least  it is up until now upon reaching the 8  000 meter mark also known as the death  zone you begin to feel uneasy no longer  is each step light and graceful now it  feels like your boots are filled with  cement you could swear your backpack is  full of bricks for a moment you think  you have lost your beanie and gloves  because your ears and fingers are ice  cold the journey suddenly becomes long  and grueling dangerous and difficult you  use every muscle in your body to climb  this thing you have to you use every bit  of mental concentration just to keep  moving you have to but why  why now could it be as simple an answer  as exhaustion accumulated fatigue how  could it be you've prepared as much as  humanly possible not just in your  training but in your life you were meant  for this moment but now the moment is  refusing your embrace nothing you could  have done could have prepared you for  this an amorphous feeling settles into  your gut you don't understand it  entirely but you anticipate something  bad is about to happen and it is at this  moment that you wish you wouldn't have  traveled alone you wish you had a  partner to reassure you that everything  would be okay you wish you had someone a  few steps in front encouraging you  forward you stop and stare ahead and  imagine it you imagine a friend to  struggle with to laugh with to succeed  with and then suddenly you don't have to  imagine any longer you see someone but  how last you heard you were the only one  to be traveling this way could it be an  illusion you squint harder and harder  trying to make sense of what you're  actually seeing 